findings.body:
This always pairs `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` set to the request’s `origin` (or `*` when absent). Any malicious site can make a credentialed cross-origin call and read the response, because the browser will treat that site’s origin as allowed.

Native/mobile clients that omit `Origin` are outside browser CORS; they do not need a wide-open allowlist. Also, `ACAO: *` with credentials is invalid per the CORS spec, so the null-origin fallback fails for credentialed browser requests while remaining unsafe whenever an Origin header is sent (e.g. mobile webviews).

Restore an explicit allowlist and only emit ACAO + credentials for known origins:

```suggestion
  const ALLOWED = new Set(["https://app.example.com"]);
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return { Vary: "Origin" };
```
